Now the concurrency test. Service-level with real H2, two threads coordinated by CountDownLatch, timeouts.

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Import(TestDatabaseCleanup.class)
class PollinationConcurrencyTest {
    @Autowired PollinationService service;
    @Autowired JdbcTemplate jdbc;

    @BeforeEach clean;

    @Test twoTechniciansSameMomentProduceSingleEffectiveOperation:
      - create batch PB-C1 (via service.createPollenBatch)
      - create inflorescence via service.createInflorescence("INF-C1", OPEN, 24)
      - ExecutorService 2 threads; CyclicBarrier or CountDownLatch ready+start
      - each calls service.pollinate(infId, "req-c-a"/"req-c-b", "PB-C1", "tech-x", IN_WINDOW)
      - collect outcomes: success (PollinationOperation) or ApiException
      - assert exactly 1 success, 1 ApiException with 409 CONFLICT
      - assert DB: count of EFFECTIVE ops = 1; inflorescence.active_operation_id = winner id

    @Test confirmAndRevokeRaceLeavesSingleTerminalState:
      - setup op
      - two threads: confirm vs revoke
      - exactly one succeeds
      - reload op: status matches winner; if confirm won → inflorescence confirmed batch set; if revoke won → active_operation_id null

    @Test retryWithSameKeyAfterFailure... covered by API idempotency. Skip.
}
```

Note service.pollinate uses @Transactional — called from separate threads, each with own transaction. Good.

Timeout: futures get(15, SECONDS), then shutdown pool.

One concern: H2 table-level locking with MVStore default — concurrent transactions updating same row: one waits for lock, gets it after other commits, sees active_operation_id set → 0 rows → conflict. Good.

